From f624eeaa0aa54defd45564ff4dbb7d0a2b711275 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:45:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 249/259] CED row copy: predicated loads ahead of every branch,
 coherent source reads

Each element branched on its row's validity and loaded inside the branch, so
an index load, the source pointer's constant load and the row load ran in
series, element after element. Loads now issue before any branch: the row
indices, then the rows, then the stores.

- Indices, whole-row pieces and gathered elements use predicated loads, which
  touch no memory when off and read as zero bits, a gathered invalid row's
  zero. They are volatile, so the compiler cannot sink a scatter's row load
  behind the store's validity test. Scattered elements load from their clamped
  row. TileLang lacks 1- and 2-byte predicated loads, and its 8-byte one
  returns a register pair that costs a copy before each index compare; the
  kernel defines those three.
- The previous vector path loaded float32 rows before checking the index, so
  the plan's -1 padding read 16 bytes before the source on every step.
- The source is not marked restrict: the read-only path a restrict source
  takes is slower for these dependent loads.
- Element layouts keep Triton's coalescing (16-byte runs of up to four
  elements, repeated a block-width apart); rows are copied in 16-byte pieces
  when the tail is packed.
- The row stride is an argument, a constant when rows are packed, so views of
  wider rows share one kernel. Whole-row pieces need 16-byte aligned rows and
  the copy needs a row to copy; both raise otherwise.

Paired against Triton at the serving layouts (eight placed graph pairs, 95%
intervals): no case slower; scatters 4 to 20% faster, whole-row gathers 1 to
7% faster, element gathers level (within 0.8%). One-row bf16 gathers, below
any CED plan, are about 5% slower.

Tests cover the serving layouts with -1 padding and out-of-range indices
across a block boundary, views of wider rows, and the preconditions.
